\lispPackageChapter{FitzCLIM/geometry}

The \texttt{FitzCLIM/geometry} package is the geometry protocol.  It
provides points, rectangles, polygons, affine transformations, and the
region operations on them: containment, union, intersection, difference,
equality, and bounding rectangles.  The protocol stays two-dimensional.
Regions, transforms, and clipping are a plane, and they stay out of the
scene graph of the 3D pane.

\section{Variables}

\lispVariableSubsection{+nowhere+}

The \texttt{+nowhere+} variable holds the empty region, an instance of the
\texttt{nowhere} class.  It contains no positions.

\lispVariableSubsection{+everywhere+}

The \texttt{+everywhere+} variable holds the region that contains every
position, an instance of the \texttt{everywhere} class.

\lispVariableSubsection{+identity-transformation+}

The \texttt{+identity-transformation+} variable holds the transformation
that leaves every position where it is.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{regionp}

The \texttt{regionp} function returns true if \texttt{object} is a region.
Points, rectangles, polygons, and region sets are regions.

\lispFunctionSubsection{nowhere-p}

The \texttt{nowhere-p} function returns true if \texttt{object} is the
empty region.

\lispFunctionSubsection{everywhere-p}

The \texttt{everywhere-p} function returns true if \texttt{object} is the
whole plane.

\lispFunctionSubsection{region-set-p}

The \texttt{region-set-p} function returns true if \texttt{object} is a
region set.

\lispFunctionSubsection{pointp}

The \texttt{pointp} function returns true if \texttt{object} is a point.

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-point}

The \texttt{make-point} function returns a point at the coordinates
\texttt{x} and \texttt{y}, which must be real.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-point \lispVar{x} \lispVar{y})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((point (make-point 3 4)))
  (pointp point)   ; Returns T
  (point-x point)  ; Returns 3
  (point-y point)) ; Returns 4
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{point-x}

The \texttt{point-x} function returns the $x$ coordinate of \texttt{point}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{point-y}

The \texttt{point-y} function returns the $y$ coordinate of \texttt{point}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{point-position}

The \texttt{point-position} function returns the $x$ and $y$ coordinates
of \texttt{point} as two values.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectanglep}

The \texttt{rectanglep} function returns true if \texttt{object} is a
rectangle.

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-rectangle}

The \texttt{make-rectangle} function returns the axis-aligned rectangle
with opposite corners \texttt{point-1} and \texttt{point-2}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-rectangle \lispVar{point-1} \lispVar{point-2})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-rectangle*}

The \texttt{make-rectangle*} function returns the axis-aligned rectangle
with corners \texttt{(x1, y1)} and \texttt{(x2, y2)}.  The corner order
does not matter.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-rectangle* \lispVar{x1} \lispVar{y1} \lispVar{x2} \lispVar{y2})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((rectangle (make-rectangle* 4 1 0 3)))
  (rectangle-min-x rectangle)   ; Returns 0
  (rectangle-min-y rectangle)   ; Returns 1
  (rectangle-max-x rectangle)   ; Returns 4
  (rectangle-max-y rectangle)   ; Returns 3
  (rectangle-width rectangle)   ; Returns 4
  (rectangle-height rectangle)) ; Returns 2
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-min-x}

The \texttt{rectangle-min-x} function returns the left edge of
\texttt{rectangle}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-min-y}

The \texttt{rectangle-min-y} function returns the top edge of
\texttt{rectangle}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-max-x}

The \texttt{rectangle-max-x} function returns the right edge of
\texttt{rectangle}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-max-y}

The \texttt{rectangle-max-y} function returns the bottom edge of
\texttt{rectangle}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-edges*}

The \texttt{rectangle-edges*} function returns the left, top, right, and
bottom edges of \texttt{rectangle} as four values.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-width}

The \texttt{rectangle-width} function returns the width of
\texttt{rectangle}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-height}

The \texttt{rectangle-height} function returns the height of
\texttt{rectangle}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-size}

The \texttt{rectangle-size} function returns the width and height of
\texttt{rectangle} as two values.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-intersection-or-nil}

The \texttt{rectangle-intersection-or-nil} function returns the
intersection of the two rectangles, or \texttt{nil} when they miss.
Touching at an edge or a corner counts as an intersection.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(rectangle-intersection-or-nil \lispVar{rectangle-1}
  \lispVar{rectangle-2})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((one (make-rectangle* 0 0 4 4))
      (two (make-rectangle* 2 2 6 6)))
  (rectangle-equal (rectangle-intersection-or-nil one two)
                   (make-rectangle* 2 2 4 4))) ; Returns T
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangle-difference-list}

The \texttt{rectangle-difference-list} function returns the parts of
\texttt{rectangle-1} that lie outside \texttt{rectangle-2}, as a list of
rectangles.  An empty list means \texttt{rectangle-2} covers
\texttt{rectangle-1}.  When the two rectangles miss, the list holds
\texttt{rectangle-1} itself.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(rectangle-difference-list \lispVar{rectangle-1}
  \lispVar{rectangle-2})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let* ((whole (make-rectangle* 0 0 10 10))
       (hole (make-rectangle* 2 2 8 8))
       (pieces (rectangle-difference-list whole hole)))
  (length pieces))              ; Returns 4
(rectangle-difference-list hole whole) ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangles-touch-horizontally-p}

The \texttt{rectangles-touch-horizontally-p} function returns true when
the two rectangles share a vertical edge and the same vertical span, so
their union is one rectangle.

\lispFunctionSubsection{rectangles-touch-vertically-p}

The \texttt{rectangles-touch-vertically-p} function returns true when the
two rectangles share a horizontal edge and the same horizontal span, so
their union is one rectangle.

\lispFunctionSubsection{join-two-rectangles}

The \texttt{join-two-rectangles} function returns one rectangle covering
both arguments.  The caller has already decided that the union is itself
a rectangle.

\lispFunctionSubsection{polygonp}

The \texttt{polygonp} function returns true if \texttt{object} is a
polygon.

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-polygon}

The \texttt{make-polygon} function returns a polygon through \texttt{points},
which must hold at least three points.  The \texttt{:convex-p} keyword
marks the polygon convex; containment of another region then tests the
corners only.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-polygon \lispVar{points} \&key \lispVar{convex-p})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((square (make-polygon* 0 0 4 0 4 3 0 3)))
  (polygonp square)                    ; Returns T
  (length (polygon-points square))     ; Returns 4
  (region-contains-position-p square 1 1)   ; Returns T
  (region-contains-position-p square 0 0)   ; Returns T
  (region-contains-position-p square 4 3)   ; Returns T
  (region-contains-position-p square 5 1))  ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-polygon*}

The \texttt{make-polygon*} function returns a polygon from the flat
coordinate list \texttt{coordinates}, which must pair $x$ and $y$.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-polygon* \&rest \lispVar{coordinates})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{polygon-points}

The \texttt{polygon-points} function returns the vertices of
\texttt{polygon}, in order.  The first vertex is not repeated at the end.

\lispFunctionSubsection{polygon-convex-p}

The \texttt{polygon-convex-p} function returns true when \texttt{polygon}
is marked convex.

\lispFunctionSubsection{map-polygon-edges}

The \texttt{map-polygon-edges} function calls \texttt{function} on each
edge of \texttt{polygon} as four coordinates, \texttt{x1 y1 x2 y2}.  The
last edge closes back to the first vertex.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(map-polygon-edges \lispVar{polygon} \lispVar{function})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{transformationp}

The \texttt{transformationp} function returns true if \texttt{object} is
a transformation.

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-transformation}

The \texttt{make-transformation} function returns the affine
transformation with the given matrix and translation.  It maps a
position by $x' = \texttt{mxx} \, x + \texttt{mxy} \, y +
\texttt{tx}$ and $y' = \texttt{myx} \, x + \texttt{myy} \, y +
\texttt{ty}$.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-transformation \lispVar{mxx} \lispVar{mxy} \lispVar{myx}
  \lispVar{myy} \lispVar{tx} \lispVar{ty})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-translation-transformation}

The \texttt{make-translation-transformation} function returns a
transformation that adds \texttt{dx} and \texttt{dy} to a position.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-translation-transformation \lispVar{dx} \lispVar{dy})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(multiple-value-bind (x y)
    (transform-position (make-translation-transformation 10 -2) 1 1)
  (list x y)) ; Returns (11 -1)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-scaling-transformation}

The \texttt{make-scaling-transformation} function returns a
transformation that scales by \texttt{sx} and \texttt{sy} about
\texttt{origin}, which defaults to \texttt{(0, 0)}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-scaling-transformation \lispVar{sx} \lispVar{sy}
  \&optional \lispVar{origin})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(multiple-value-bind (x y)
    (transform-position (make-scaling-transformation 2 3) 4 5)
  (list x y)) ; Returns (8 15)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-rotation-transformation}

The \texttt{make-rotation-transformation} function returns a
transformation that rotates counterclockwise by \texttt{angle} radians
about \texttt{origin}, which defaults to \texttt{(0, 0)}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(make-rotation-transformation \lispVar{angle}
  \&optional \lispVar{origin})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{compose-transformations}

The \texttt{compose-transformations} function returns the
transformation that applies \texttt{transformation-2} first and
\texttt{transformation-1} second.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(compose-transformations \lispVar{transformation-1}
  \lispVar{transformation-2})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{invert-transformation}

The \texttt{invert-transformation} function returns the inverse of
\texttt{transformation}.  It signals \texttt{SINGULAR-TRANSFORMATION}
when the transformation has no inverse.

\lispFunctionSubsection{transform-position}

The \texttt{transform-position} function returns the image of
\texttt{(x, y)} under \texttt{transformation}, as two values.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(transform-position \lispVar{transformation} \lispVar{x}
  \lispVar{y})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(multiple-value-bind (x y) (transform-position +identity-transformation+ 3 4)
  (list x y)) ; Returns (3 4)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{untransform-position}

The \texttt{untransform-position} function returns the position that
\texttt{transformation} maps to \texttt{(x, y)}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(untransform-position \lispVar{transformation} \lispVar{x}
  \lispVar{y})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(multiple-value-bind (x y)
    (untransform-position (make-translation-transformation 5 7) 8 9)
  (list x y)) ; Returns (3 2)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{transformation-rectilinear-p}

The \texttt{transformation-rectilinear-p} function returns true when
\texttt{transformation} maps axis-aligned rectangles to axis-aligned
rectangles.  A rotation of a quarter turn does; a shear does not.

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(transformation-rectilinear-p +identity-transformation+) ; Returns T
(transformation-rectilinear-p (make-transformation 0 -1 1 0 0 0)) ; Returns T
(transformation-rectilinear-p (make-transformation 1 -1 1 1 0 0)) ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle} function returns the smallest
axis-aligned rectangle that covers \texttt{region}.  The empty region
and the whole plane have no such rectangle, and the function signals
\texttt{BOUNDING-RECTANGLE-OF-NOWHERE} for them.

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(bounding-rectangle (make-polygon* 0 1 3 4 1 2))
  ; Returns the rectangle from (0, 1) to (3, 4)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle-min-x}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-min-x} function returns the left edge of
the bounding rectangle of \texttt{region}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle-min-y}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-min-y} function returns the top edge of
the bounding rectangle of \texttt{region}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle-max-x}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-max-x} function returns the right edge of
the bounding rectangle of \texttt{region}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle-max-y}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-max-y} function returns the bottom edge
of the bounding rectangle of \texttt{region}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle-width}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-width} function returns the width of the
bounding rectangle of \texttt{region}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle-height}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-height} function returns the height of
the bounding rectangle of \texttt{region}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{bounding-rectangle-size}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-size} function returns the width and
height of the bounding rectangle of \texttt{region} as two values.

\section{Generics}

\lispGenericSubsection{region-contains-position-p}

The \texttt{region-contains-position-p} generic returns true when the
position \texttt{(x, y)} lies in \texttt{region}.  Boundaries count as
inside.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(region-contains-position-p \lispVar{region} \lispVar{x}
  \lispVar{y})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(region-contains-position-p (make-rectangle* 0 1 4 3) 0 1) ; Returns T
(region-contains-position-p (make-rectangle* 0 1 4 3) 2 2) ; Returns T
(region-contains-position-p (make-rectangle* 0 1 4 3) -1 2) ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{region-contains-region-p}

The \texttt{region-contains-region-p} generic returns true when every
point of \texttt{region2} lies in \texttt{region1}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(region-contains-region-p \lispVar{region1} \lispVar{region2})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(region-contains-region-p +everywhere+ +nowhere+) ; Returns T
(region-contains-region-p +nowhere+ +everywhere+) ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{region-equal}

The \texttt{region-equal} generic returns true when \texttt{region1} and
\texttt{region2} contain the same points.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(region-equal \lispVar{region1} \lispVar{region2})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(region-equal (make-point 3 4) (make-point 3 4)) ; Returns T
(region-equal (make-point 3 4) (make-point 3 5)) ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{region-intersects-region-p}

The \texttt{region-intersects-region-p} generic returns true when
\texttt{region1} and \texttt{region2} have at least one point in common.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(region-intersects-region-p \lispVar{region1} \lispVar{region2})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(region-intersects-region-p (make-rectangle* 0 0 2 2)
                            (make-rectangle* 2 0 5 2)) ; Returns T
(region-intersects-region-p +everywhere+ +nowhere+) ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{region-union}

The \texttt{region-union} generic returns the region that contains a
point exactly when \texttt{region} or one of \texttt{regions} contains
it.  Rectangles whose union is a single rectangle are joined; points
inside other members are dropped.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(region-union \lispVar{region} \&rest \lispVar{regions})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((left (make-rectangle* 0 0 2 2))
      (right (make-rectangle* 2 0 5 2))
      (joined (region-union left right)))
  (list (rectanglep joined) (rectangle-width joined))) ; Returns (T 5)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{region-intersection}

The \texttt{region-intersection} generic returns the region of points
that lie in \texttt{region} and in every region in \texttt{regions}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(region-intersection \lispVar{region} \&rest \lispVar{regions})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(region-equal (region-intersection (make-rectangle* 0 0 4 4)
                                   (make-rectangle* 2 2 6 6))
              (make-rectangle* 2 2 4 4)) ; Returns T
(region-equal +nowhere+ (region-intersection +nowhere+ +everywhere+)) ; Returns T
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{region-difference}

The \texttt{region-difference} generic returns the points of
\texttt{region1} that do not lie in \texttt{region2}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(region-difference \lispVar{region1} \lispVar{region2})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((rest (region-difference (make-rectangle* 0 0 4 2)
                               (make-rectangle* 1 0 2 2))))
  (list (region-set-p rest)
        (region-contains-position-p rest 0.5 1)
        (region-contains-position-p rest 1.5 1)
        (region-contains-position-p rest 3 1)))
  ; Returns (T T NIL T)
(region-equal +nowhere+ (region-difference +everywhere+ +everywhere+)) ; Returns T
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{bounding-rectangle*}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle*} generic returns the left, top, right,
and bottom edges of the smallest axis-aligned rectangle that covers
\texttt{region}, as four values.  It signals
\texttt{BOUNDING-RECTANGLE-OF-NOWHERE} for the empty region and the
whole plane.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(bounding-rectangle* \lispVar{region})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(multiple-value-bind (left top right bottom)
    (bounding-rectangle* (make-polygon* 0 1 3 4 1 2))
  (list left top right bottom)) ; Returns (0 1 3 4)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{transform-region}

The \texttt{transform-region} generic returns the image of
\texttt{region} under \texttt{transformation}.  A rectangle stays a
rectangle when the transformation is rectilinear, and becomes a convex
polygon when it is not.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(transform-region \lispVar{transformation} \lispVar{region})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((shift (make-translation-transformation 10 0)))
  (region-equal (transform-region shift (make-rectangle* 0 0 4 3))
                (make-rectangle* 10 0 14 3))) ; Returns T
(let* ((shear (make-transformation 1 0.5 0 1 0 0))
       (image (transform-region shear (make-rectangle* 0 0 2 2))))
  (list (polygonp image) (polygon-convex-p image))) ; Returns (T T)
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{region}

The \texttt{region} type is the abstract base of the geometry protocol.
A region is a set of points in the plane.  The subclasses are
\texttt{nowhere}, \texttt{everywhere}, \texttt{point},
\texttt{rectangle}, \texttt{polygon}, and \texttt{region-set}.

\lispTypeSubsection{nowhere}

The \texttt{nowhere} type is the empty region.  It contains no
positions.

\lispTypeSubsection{everywhere}

The \texttt{everywhere} type is the whole plane.

\lispTypeSubsection{point}

The \texttt{point} type is a single position in the plane, stored as two
real coordinates in \texttt{standard-point}.

\lispTypeSubsection{rectangle}

The \texttt{rectangle} type is an axis-aligned rectangle.  Top is the
smaller $y$ coordinate, and bottom is the larger one.  The boundary is
inside the rectangle.

\lispTypeSubsection{polygon}

The \texttt{polygon} type is a filled polygon.  The boundary is inside.
\texttt{standard-polygon} stores the vertices as a list of points and
keeps a convexity mark.

\lispTypeSubsection{region-set}

The \texttt{region-set} type is a region made of member regions.  A
position is inside the set when it is inside some member.
\texttt{standard-region-set} stores the members as a list, in no
particular order.

\lispTypeSubsection{standard-region-difference}

The \texttt{standard-region-difference} type is the region of points
that lie in one region and not in another, stored as the minuend and the
subtrahend.  A difference of rectangles that splits into rectangles is
stored as those rectangles instead.

\lispTypeSubsection{transformation}

The \texttt{transformation} type is a mapping of positions to
positions.  \texttt{standard-transformation} is the affine form:
$x' = \texttt{mxx} \, x + \texttt{mxy} \, y + \texttt{tx}$ and
$y' = \texttt{myx} \, x + \texttt{myy} \, y + \texttt{ty}$.

\section{Conditions}

\subsection{The \texttt{singular-transformation} Condition}

The \texttt{singular-transformation} condition signals that a
transformation has no inverse.  Its linear part collapses the plane onto
a line or a point.

\subsection{The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-of-nowhere} Condition}

The \texttt{bounding-rectangle-of-nowhere} condition signals that a
bounding rectangle was requested for the empty region or the whole
plane, which have no edges.
